 \section{Objective and Relationship to the Research Questions}
-HANDOVER tests whether paired video and force--torque signals can identify the recorded outcome of a completed human--robot handover. The single-input predictors address representation choice (SQ1), and averaging their probabilities tests the contribution of the second input on the same trials (SQ2). For SQ3, controlled blur, sensor noise, and single-stream fallback show how the baselines behave when input quality or availability changes. For SQ4, class-specific decisions at a fixed threshold complement the ranking scores. Missing participant identifiers limit the uncertainty analysis and prevent participant-disjoint fitting of the proposed learned fusion model.\ev{HANDOVER study interpretation}{\EvidenceRef{E086}{}}
-
-The HANDOVER dataset paper compared video, force/torque and gripper inputs for completed-trial failure detection \citep{Thoduka2024Handover}. Its five-class I3D model achieved 69.1\% accuracy with video and force/torque, falling to 67.9\% when the gripper input was added. The authors also investigated temporal action modelling with auxiliary action supervision. The binary experiment here evaluates a different target and uses AP, which precludes a numerical comparison with those five-class accuracies.
