 \section{Study Objective and Relationship to the Research Questions}
-For SQ1, the image and sensor experiments assessed whether frozen visual features and temporal sensor representations could reproduce a specified target. For SQ2, the principal fusion comparison combined context, root-mean-square (RMS) vibration and condition indicators, all available at inference. It did not test joint image--sensor inference. For SQ3, a predictor that already returns nearly constant values can appear insensitive to corruption, so its clean-input error matters. For SQ4, the training-mean comparison asks whether measured sensor inputs improved prediction over a rule that ignores them at inference. It cannot establish whether the photographic target measures damage. That question requires a physical reference independent of the rule.\ev{PHM target and deployment inputs}{\EvidenceRef{E021}{}}
-
-\section{Dataset Overview and Acquisition Structure}
-The retained population contains EXP-A runs 1--5, EXP-B runs 1--7 and EXP-F runs 1--8: twenty nominal run units across three experiments. A run can contain multiple tooth photographs, camera-sequence views, high-frequency vibration members, low-frequency context measurements and condition-indicator records. An image, a minute-level feature row and a run are consequently different observational units. Repeated views and long sensor sequences increase the number of stored observations without increasing the number of independently acquired experiments to the same extent.
-
-Experiment and run identifiers are retained through derived manifests. Tooth identity and image role define target generation, whereas archive/member identity and measurement provenance define sensor features. Their correspondence is principally at run level. This supports joining measurements to a post-run proxy, rather than asserting hardware-synchronized image/sensor events or prospective image availability. Evidence covers these manifests and inspected implementation, with incomplete independent reinspection of raw archive contents.\ev{PHM experiments and acquisition sources}{\EvidenceRef{E020}{}}
-
-\section{Dataset Challenges and Supervision Limitations}
-The target depends on acquisition geometry, illumination, texture and image-processing choices as well as possible surface damage. In EXP-A and EXP-B, tooth indices 1--4 and 5--28 had different image-role coverage. Because tooth identity and view role were confounded, comparing their candidate-area percentages could mix material differences with acquisition differences. The v3 exclusion described below addresses this particular problem; it does not independently validate the retained twenty-four teeth.
-
-The organizers reported possible overlap for EXP-A Run-2. A separate analysis addressed equality between derived sequences. A tutorial reports equality of 372 feature rows across 72 derived spectral features for two runs. That is evidence about its extracted representation, not byte-level proof that complete raw archives are identical. Both nominal runs remain visible in the historical analysis. The small number of independent experiments, unequal run lengths and changing acquisition conditions limit broad generalization even when train/test identifiers do not overlap.\ev{Organizer-reported overlap in EXP-A}{\EvidenceRef{E020}{}}\ev{Derived spectral diagnostic}{\EvidenceRef{E037}{}}
-
-\section{Preparation, Exploratory Analysis, and Pre-Checks}
-Preparation distinguished structural eligibility from scientific validity. Source manifests retained experiment, run, tooth and image identities; sensor inspection considered channel presence, units, finite values, temporal coverage and operating-state eligibility. The early EXP-A exploration used runs 1, 3 and 5 and remains a deliberately narrower historical analysis, not evidence that every later included run received the same exploratory scrutiny. Its protected outputs were not rewritten to match the later all-run population.
-
-Minute-level representations summarize process and sensor variables instead of passing the entire waveform to the principal model. Timestamp and coverage checks support sequence assembly, while aggregation can discard transients. The high-frequency tutorial has different representation provenance. Duplicate diagnostics, missingness masks and exclusions therefore remain tied to their producing configurations; combining them would define a different dataset from that which generated the historical predictions.\ev{PHM domain implementation}{\EvidenceRef{E034}{}}
-
-\section{Task Formulation and Target-Version History}
-The current-end-of-run target is a percentage of a fixed visible-flank region classified as candidate area. It is not remaining useful life, a native failure class, or a calibrated physical spall-area measurement. A run-level target aggregates the largest three tooth values. Raw target values and a causal monotonic transformation are separate outcomes; monotonic processing cannot serve as independent evidence that the raw measurements track deterioration.
