-\section{Additional Diagnostic Experiments and Evaluation Sensitivity}
-Supplementary analyses investigate why the original routes were insufficient, without replacing the principal comparison. The HF/spectral tutorial reports MAE 1.2203 for its full 72-feature model against a v2 constant MAE 0.6797 on eight EXP-F runs. Reduced-feature variants performed better than that full model but none beat its constant. Repeatedly inspecting feature subsets on the same test split makes this a development diagnostic, not independent confirmation of the selected subset. Its strong causal language about overfitting or complete archive duplication exceeds what feature tables alone establish and is not adopted here.
-
-The four-arm study compares group regressors, concatenation, affine late fusion and context-conditioned embeddings, but its preprocessing-isolation concern prevents merging its results with principal LOEO. Raw-vibration and tooth-sector screens, AnomalyDINO sweeps, odd/even views, synthetic controls, teacher/oracle constructions and monotonic rescoring address separate uncertainties. An oracle or target-derived synthetic feature can demonstrate pipeline capacity without demonstrating deployable information. Detailed diagnostic tables belong in Appendix D with their own target and exposure labels.\ev{HF diagnostic report}{\EvidenceRef{E037}{}}\ev{Four-arm implementation}{\EvidenceRef{E029}{}}
