-\section{Motivation and Intended Comparison}
-In MSM milling, the logistic sensor model ranked abnormal intervals more accurately than the stacker (F2) that combined its predictions with video. Both models predicted the same labelled intervals under the same grouping rules. Moving from assembly to milling changed the measurements, prediction interval and target.
-
-The study connects the original questions in a controlled sequence. The frozen CNN and interval statistics test a lightweight representation choice (SQ1); averaging, original F2 and supplementary quality gates test fusion alternatives (SQ2); controlled input stresses and explicit absence fallbacks address a bounded part of robustness (SQ3). Target semantics, grouped assessment, probability quality and the distinction between software feasibility and predictive value address SQ4. None of these comparisons establishes early warning before an abnormal cut or transfer without target-domain fitting.
-
-The pretrained-initialization comparison covered three fixed training seeds and the specified target, grouping and corruption conditions. An initial development run tested the implementation on a small sample. Performance estimates came from the subsequent held-out intervals that met the timing and input-coverage criteria.\ev{MSM experimental design}{\EvidenceRef{E079}{}}
-
-A close manufacturing comparator illustrates why the unimodal reference is essential. The tool-wear study in \citet{P045} combines post-pass tool photographs with acoustic information and reports its proposed MAE below a visual baseline, while other multimodal architectures perform substantially worse than that same visual baseline. Its wear-regression task, observation schedule and incompletely reported tool-split coverage differ from MSM. It motivates testing the contribution of a second stream; it does not supply a transferable numerical expectation of improvement.
