-\section{Dataset Overview and Target Semantics}
-The source dataset combines laboratory and industrial metal-cutting observations \citep{Kim2026MSM}. It is a data descriptor, rather than a published unimodal-versus-fusion benchmark. Its streams have different sampling and refresh rates, and the smartphone camera is available only in the laboratory material. The evaluated release is the pinned Kaggle version~2 \citep{MSMKaggle2025}. The retained laboratory material covers VM20i and VMX30Ui; the industrial VF10 component is outside this visual comparison because it does not supply the corresponding video. The source overview describes 56 laboratory experiments. Membership in that inventory is distinct from admission to the final paired cohort.
-
-Native labels distinguish stable normal cutting, intervention-worthy abnormal cutting, and stable cutting with a defective tool. Their values are respectively 0, 1 and 2. The primary binary outcome is label~1 against labels~0 and~2. A negative outcome therefore means stable cutting under this definition; it does not certify a healthy tool. Expert annotation uses process behavior, sound and measurements, so the label is an observed process judgment rather than an independently instrumented future-failure endpoint. Relabeling every defective-tool interval as abnormal would change the task. The study instead repeats the pipeline after excluding label~2 and retains that as a distinct target sensitivity.\ev{MSM target definition}{\EvidenceRef{E074}{}}
-
-Of 2,512 native labeled intervals, 1,895 satisfy the joint video and numerical-input requirements, including 336 positives. The remaining 617 retain their original labels and explicit exclusion reasons. One prediction refers to one completed cutting interval. Neither the whole interval nor its final label is shortened into a prediction horizon that would establish advance warning.
