-\section{Dataset Challenges}
-Milling video can show the machine and process context while coolant, enclosure geometry and occlusion conceal the tool--workpiece interaction. Successful decoding is therefore insufficient evidence that the labeled abnormality is optically observable. A visual classifier may respond to operating context, lighting or experiment-specific appearance rather than the underlying cutting event. The substantial visual training--assessment gap reported below is compatible with this concern, but does not identify its cause.
-
-The machine stream is also an observation of the process rather than direct, instantaneous ground truth. Aggregated electrical, load and vibration-related measurements can describe an interval while smoothing short events. Their timing and update cadence limit any claim about event onset or control latency. Group identity creates a further difficulty: experiment folders provide a useful split boundary, but they are not verified identities for physical tools, workpieces or sessions. These limits motivate both the technical gates and the stricter grouping sensitivities.\ev{MSM validation limits}{\EvidenceRef{E075}{}}
-
-\section{Preparation and Pre-Checks}
-Preparation admits examples only when both modalities meet a fixed input contract. The dominant exclusion is lack of validated acoustic timing support, followed by rejected or insufficient alignment and numerical gaps. These are technical exclusions, not removals based on model correctness. The exclusion record identifies intervals removed before modelling. Successful decoding alone does not establish that an anomaly is visible.
